//
// @library      Blueprint Ephemeris Library
// @description  Complete planetary ephemeris in a single library. Computes geocentric
//               and heliocentric positions for all 10 solar system bodies: Sun, Moon,
//               Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto.
//               Provides longitude, declination, speed, and lunar node positions.
//
// @author       BlueprintResearch (Javonnii)
// @license      MIT License - Free to use with attribution
// @repository   https://github.com/javonniicurry/Planetary_Ephemeris
//
// @theories     VSOP87D — Mercury through Neptune (Bretagnon & Francou, 1988)
//               ELP2000-82 — Moon (Chapront-Touzé & Chapront, 1983; Meeus truncation)
//               Meeus Series — Pluto (Meeus, "Astronomical Algorithms", 1998)
//
// @accuracy     All planets validated against JPL DE440 via Skyfield (2000-2050):
//               Sun: 0.004° · Mercury-Neptune: 0.005-0.017° · Moon: 0.062° · Pluto: 0.059°
//               All bodies < 0.1° RMS — no correction factors needed.
//
// @v2_changes   • Consolidated from 11 separate libraries into 1 (4,300 → ~3,100 lines, 28% reduction)
//               • Fixed VSOP87D L1[0] precession coefficients for all 8 planets
//                 (was using VSOP87B values — missing +0.24382 rad/millennium general precession)
//               • Error reduced from ~0.75° to < 0.1° for all VSOP87 planets
//               • Single import — no dependency chain, faster execution
//               • Moon functions accept raw Unix time (no manual Julian century conversion)
//               • Lunar node functions renamed for clarity (north/south explicit)
//
// @time_scale   All exported functions accept Unix timestamp in milliseconds
//               (use TradingView's built-in 'time' variable directly)
//               Internal: Julian millennia (VSOP87), Julian centuries (Moon/Pluto)
//
// @reference    Meeus, Jean. "Astronomical Algorithms" (2nd Ed., 1998)
//               Bretagnon & Francou. "Planetary Theories in rectangular and spherical
//               variables. VSOP87 Solutions" (Astronomy & Astrophysics, 1988)
//               Chapront-Touzé & Chapront. "ELP2000-82" (1983)
//               Miller, Greg. github.com/gmiller123456/vsop87-multilang (validation reference)
//
// @open_source  This library is part of an open-source alternative to
//               proprietary astronomical libraries. Study, modify, and
//               share freely. We believe knowledge of the cosmos belongs
//               to everyone.
//
